.TH CHTTPCLI_SET_ENGINE_LOGGER 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpcli_set_engine_logger \- set the logger for the diagnostics of the async engine of chttpclient
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpcli_set_engine_logger(clog cl);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function derives a child logger from cl, with the added field
component=http-client-engine, and installs that child logger as the
diagnostics logger of the shared async engine of Tier 2 and Tier 3. It
closes the engine logger that was there before, if there was one.

You can call this function at any time, and it takes effect at once.
The engine keeps the child logger across every stop and restart of the
engine, until another call to this function replaces it.

chttpclient_do_async, chttpclient_do_async_streaming and the
pooled-sync wrappers on top of them (chttpclient_do_pooled and
chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming) share one ccol_event_loop reactor.
That reactor covers the whole process and every chttpcli instance in
it, the default client included, and the library starts it only when
it first needs it. This function captures the diagnostics of that
reactor, such as a failure of a TLS handshake or an error on a
connect. It is separate from the logger of each client, and also from
the reactor of chttpsvr_set_engine_logger: chttpserver and chttpclient
each own a fully independent static reactor, and one process can run
both at the same time.

While no logger from this function is installed, the library opens a
fallback logger each time that the engine starts, and closes it when
the engine stops. That fallback logger writes to fd 2 at the level
CLOG_FATAL, and this engine never logs its own diagnostics above
CLOG_INFO, so the fallback logger is silent until you use this
function to install a logger that says more.

cl must not be CLOG_INVALID. The caller keeps the ownership of cl, and
may close it after this call returns: the child logger shares the
destination of cl, and clogger keeps that destination open while any
logger that shares it is open. The engine owns the child logger, and
the caller must not close it. The library closes it when another call
replaces it, and at the exit of the process or the unload of the
library when no engine runs then.

For a logger from clog_open_fd or clog_open_fd_mp, that destination is
the state of the logger, not the file descriptor: such a logger never
owns its fd, and no clog_close closes it; see clog_open_fd(3). The
engine keeps writing to that fd after you close cl, so the fd itself
must stay open until another call to this function replaces the engine
logger, or until the process exits.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, if the call succeeds.
ccol_invalid_args, if cl is CLOG_INVALID.
ccol_not_enough_memory, if the library cannot allocate the child
    logger.
.fi
